**Amendment (Bounded transport evidence)** · `amend:interchange:transport-evidence` The separate `Value::from_transport_bytes` entry implements (`[ROOT-rule:api:message-decoding]`) through the same private grammar walk as the canonical entries. Its finite caller declarations have no deployment defaults; the operational API limits remain pending. Allowed outer variants and unchanged embedded bytes have (`claim:encoding:transport-variants-preserve-values`); malformed and unsupported inputs have paired controls in (`claim:encoding:transport-refusals-retain-valid-controls`). Transport buffers grow geometrically, capped by the declared count for definite containers; relocation work and the full replacement capacity are charged before each reservation. Actual decoder reservation calls remain observable on refusal in (`claim:encoding:transport-limits-precede-reservations`), and (`claim:encoding:strict-entry-selects-strict-policy`) names the strict policy seam. The unchanged canonical corpus remains required. This scopes (`[ROOT-verif:xchg:bounded-transport]`) to transient values; it establishes neither a served API nor membership of a carried document.

**Definition (Control schema carriage)** · `def:interchange:control-schema` The separate control_schema module implements the closed envelope of (`[ROOT-dec:api:schema]`) for org.peer-network.test.api-control-schema at major one and minor zero, with an unsigned patch. It checks the complete original canonical bytes, exact role, edition and sole text field; it neither parses SDL nor admits an executable or served edition. (`claim:documents:control-envelope-refusals`) compares each refusal with its named class and the independently declared closed theory. (`claim:documents:control-source-retention`) carries valid UTF-8 only: invalid text, including a byte encoding of an unpaired surrogate, is refused as InvalidUtf8. Valid text is retained without Unicode, escape, line-ending or final-newline normalization: quotes, backslash, tab, NUL, composed and decomposed Unicode, LF, CR and CRLF remain distinct content. Test inputs are not publications at assigned coordinates. The input/record drift check is (`claim:documents:control-source-record-agreement`) and the generated artifact comparison is (`claim:documents:control-artifact-fixed-point`).

The authored record-018 template is the source authority under (`[ROOT-amend:api:test-schema-plan]`); packages/interchange/schema/control.graphql is its byte-exact committed input copy. (`claim:documents:control-source-record-agreement`) compares both that template's fenced payload and its generated render with the input, including the final LF. Run cargo run --locked --profile gate -p pn-interchange --example control_schema -- . write from the workspace root to regenerate packages/interchange/schema/control.cbor, or replace write with check to compare without writing. The example is the registered artifact producer; its explicit root is a runtime input. Its argument handling, publication path and comparison mode are shared directly with the fixed-point control, which executes both modes in a fresh runtime directory, checks refusal of an absent or changed artifact, and verifies that comparison does not repair bytes. (`claim:documents:control-artifact-fixed-point`) compares the committed bytes with production regeneration, independent expected bytes and decoded source on every suite execution. The artifact is its exact bytes and has no digest.

The independent corpus at packages/interchange/tests/corpus/control-schema.json has thirty-seven distinct byte inputs in authored, unsorted case order. Its producer packages/interchange/tests/control_vectors.py uses its own RFC 8949 integer, array, map and UTF-8 framing, calling no Rust implementation or helper. The hash audit's control-schema-vectors family regenerates and compares the entire corpus before accounting its byte strings; the Rust suite reads that committed corpus and invokes no interpreter. The ten positive inputs include the admitted source and a maximal patch; the twenty-seven negatives separately vary roles, editions, content and original canonical bytes, including duplicate and unsorted keys, indefinite length, a non-map document, a non-integer key, tagged content and both unpaired-surrogate forms. This finite conformance and robustness evidence establishes no SDL semantics, register agreement, measured bounds, resolver or service. The control register and both node-role coordinates remain without artifacts.
